  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251575/assets/b2c3f59df41cb406ddfc41eea81a979dec578ba990032f0ff4f2f9792f58c8b2.png)\n\nThe vector $a$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $-\\frac{3}{4}a$ and $2a?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that both $-\\frac{3}{4}a$ and $2a$ must be parallel to $a.$\n\nNow, for the vector $-\\frac{3}{4}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-\\frac{3}{4}a$ is $\\frac{3}{4}$ of the magnitude of $a.$\n\n- Vectors $a$ and $-\\frac{3}{4}a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-\\frac{3}{4}$ is negative.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251575/assets/13e4de227344b9eb7d1867cca0d05d9bcb647662a0c732398b42277ac379d361.png)\n\n \n\nNext, for the vector $2a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $2a$ is twice the magnitude of $a.$\n\n- Vectors $a$ and $2a$ have the same direction since the factor $2$ is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251575/assets/13ca18bebfdb3c738d0a1198d0ce4235bfd77185bcf232f79f9ba089ce3b6d38.png)",
